Avoid getting caught in the cycle of explaining, threatening, and personal attacks.
Step One Calming Your Own Emotions. When we feel an urge to attack the other person internally, we need to try our best to suppress it. For example, we can achieve this through several methods mentioned in the story:
Correcting misjudgments of the facts; Distracting yourself through deep breathing; Or simply restraining the fist you want to throw; Or any other method that might be effective. During communication with others, every time you feel this urge reignite internally, you must review this step in your mind. Completing this step only takes a few seconds, making it perhaps the most difficult and most important step. If you don&rsquo;t have the urge to attack the other person, that is naturally better. You can then proceed directly to the next step.
Step Two Calming Others&rsquo; Emotions. If the person talking to you can remain calm, that&rsquo;s easy; you can skip this step directly. But what if the other party is not calm? The answer is &ldquo;do nothing,&rdquo; or rather, &ldquo;do almost nothing.&rdquo; Especially do not tell the other person, &ldquo;Calm down!&rdquo; or &ldquo;Getting angry is useless!&rdquo; At this time, you should follow the principle of &ldquo;not contradicting, not judging.&rdquo; Specifically, how do you achieve this? You can reply to the other person using words like &ldquo;agree,&rdquo; &ldquo;okay,&rdquo; &ldquo;yes,&rdquo; or &ldquo;that&rsquo;s right.&rdquo; These words can convey the following message to the other person: &ldquo;What you are saying, and the way you choose to say it, naturally makes sense to you. I am willing to discuss this second step: Calming Others&rsquo; Emotions. If the person talking to you can remain calm, that&rsquo;s easy; you can skip this step directly. But what if the other party is not calm? The answer is &lsquo;do nothing,&rsquo; or rather, &lsquo;do almost nothing.&rsquo; Especially do not tell the other person, &lsquo;Calm down!&rsquo; or &lsquo;Getting angry is useless!&rsquo; At this time, you should follow the principle of &rsquo;not contradicting, not judging.&rsquo; Specifically, how do you achieve this? You can reply to the other person using words like &lsquo;agree,&rsquo; &lsquo;okay,&rsquo; &lsquo;yes,&rsquo; or &rsquo;that&rsquo;s right.&rsquo; These words can convey the following message to the other person: &lsquo;What you are saying, and the way you choose to say it, naturally makes sense to you. I am willing to discuss this issue with you.&rsquo;&rdquo;
By doing this, you will achieve astonishing results: the other person&rsquo;s emotions will initially fluctuate, but then gradually stabilize until they finally calm down. Achieving this step only requires the moment you say, &ldquo;I agree.&rdquo; If you successfully control your own emotions and successfully calm the other person&rsquo;s emotions, you can proceed to the next step.
Step Three Try to Understand Others Rather Than Having Others Understand You. How do you achieve this step? The simplest method is &ldquo;asking the other person questions.&rdquo; The most effective question is: &ldquo;Why do you disagree with my point of view?&rdquo; After that, you must listen to their answer and try to view the issue from their perspective, even putting yourself in their shoes and considering their interests.
Strive to understand and accept others&rsquo; views, rather than forcing your own views onto them. You must learn to find &ldquo;consensus&rdquo; between both parties in the words they use when explaining, and willingly accept the other person&rsquo;s viewpoint. This is a prerequisite for both parties reaching &ldquo;consensus.&rdquo;
At this point, you need to have more &ldquo;self-interest&rdquo; regarding others: make yourself consider the other person&rsquo;s interests more! Once you understand the other person&rsquo;s thoughts, the solution will appear on its own, and the disagreement will be resolved easily. However, generally speaking, achieving this is not enough; you need to continue to complete the next step&hellip;
Step Four Restating Others&rsquo; Words to Let Them Know &ldquo;You Have Understood Their Point of View.&rdquo; If you want others to listen to your point of view, you first need to let the other person speak. Then, you explain the other person&rsquo;s view in your own words. Afterward, you ask the other person, &ldquo;Did I say it correctly?&rdquo;
This way, you will achieve unexpected magical results—the other person will raise their eyebrows, show a satisfied smile, and exclaim, &ldquo;Yes, that&rsquo;s right!&rdquo; After that, they will close their mouth and listen to you speak.
Restating has two benefits: First, you can check whether you truly understood the other person&rsquo;s point of view; second, it lets your conversational partner know that they have been understood, which in turn dispels the urge to continue arguing. However, there is a prerequisite for achieving this step—don&rsquo;t let your previous efforts go down the drain because you used the wrong word. This word is the keyword for the next step. The use of this word is also an art.
Step Five Presenting Your Own Viewpoint Using Parallel Words Rather Than Making the Two Opposing Views Confrontational. How do you achieve this step? You can use the following words, such as: &ldquo;As for me,&rdquo; &ldquo;For me,&rdquo; &ldquo;Meanwhile,&rdquo; &ldquo;From my perspective,&rdquo; etc., instead of the extremely rigid &ldquo;That&rsquo;s right, but&hellip;&rdquo;
Then, after both parties have clarified the other&rsquo;s viewpoint, ask yourself, &ldquo;How can the other person&rsquo;s needs be met while simultaneously achieving my own goals?&rdquo; By doing this, you can utilize the intelligence of both parties to seek a solution. Next, we begin entering Step Six.
Step Six Proposing Solutions: Adopt a Possible Win-Win Solution. If we cannot find a feasible solution ourselves, try asking others if they have any good ideas. Then, we can discuss it together.
If no win-win method can be found, we can use a compromise approach to solve the problem. People often accept a compromise because it at least establishes a friendly and cooperative relationship.
So, what if even a compromise solution doesn&rsquo;t exist? This situation is very rare, but not impossible. At this time, you can negotiate with the other party and spend time together exploring possible ways out. Even if a solution is not ultimately found, at least a good relationship is maintained. This positive relationship is crucial for achieving future collaboration.

· False Dichotomy We cannot assume that one must be correct just because there are only two choices presented. Many things are not &ldquo;either/or&rdquo; or &ldquo;black/white.&rdquo; &ldquo;Since you don&rsquo;t support free trade, you must support protectionism!&rdquo;
· Middle Ground Many people like to take the middle ground between two choices because it avoids either extreme, so sometimes this approach is called moderation (or the Doctrine of the Mean). &ldquo;The junior high teacher believes that the school should arrange a fixed timetable. But the parents believe that students should be able to choose courses freely. So the best way is to combine the two.&rdquo;
· Hodgepodge Putting together locally correct things does not necessarily result in a correct outcome. &ldquo;Brad is a nice young man, and Janet is an excellent lady; they must be a perfect pair.&rdquo; Marriage is not as simple as &ldquo;1+1=2.&rdquo; Two excellent people may not necessarily become a pair of excellent spouses, so local correctness does not necessarily mean overall correctness.
· Partial Error Just because the whole is correct does not mean that every part of it is correct. For example, a team is great, which doesn&rsquo;t mean that all the players on that team are equally great. &ldquo;Because the car is heavy, all the accessories on the car must be heavy.&rdquo; Clearly, this statement is not true!
· Fallacy of Continuity Sometimes people feel that some differences are too small to be worth paying attention to, which leads to the Fallacy of Continuity. &ldquo;Learn one new word every day to improve your vocabulary. Take a medium-sized dictionary and start from the beginning. If you memorize one new word every day, eventually you will reach the last page. And more importantly, you will learn almost all the important words in English. But how many people can achieve this?&rdquo;
· Attacking the Point Many people believe that something that has been generalized is easily refuted, because finding just one exception is enough. For example, a cinema claims that children under 18 are not allowed entry, but this doesn&rsquo;t mean that parents cannot bring a 7-month-old baby to watch a movie. Student A: &ldquo;It is well known that smoking shortens human lifespan.&rdquo; Student B: &ldquo;Yes, but my great-grandfather smoked a pack a day and is still over 90 years old and doing fine. How should we explain that?&rdquo; Finding an exception does not mean the generalization is wrong—everything has exceptions, but exceptions are always exceptions, just low-probability events.
· Distortion What is meant by distortion is deliberately twisting the opponent&rsquo;s viewpoint in order to achieve the goal of refuting the opponent. Proponent: &ldquo;The only way to improve the educational level of developing countries is to provide more material support, such as textbooks.&rdquo; Opponent: &ldquo;Are you saying that no matter how many trees are cut down, you still have to print more textbooks?&rdquo;
· Faulty Analogy We cannot assume that two things are similar (or dissimilar) in other aspects just because they are similar (or dissimilar) in one or a few aspects. &ldquo;Speaking of artificial bait, my favorite is Rapala bait. This summer, every time I use it, I catch many smallmouth bass, so I will definitely use it when fishing for trout in the fall.&rdquo;
· Causal Error When we arbitrarily attribute something to a certain aspect, we are committing a causal error. &ldquo;I heard that rich people work hard, so I must work hard to become rich.&rdquo; Indeed, working hard may make a person wealthy (at least from a financial perspective), but it is only one factor. We cannot arbitrarily assume that working hard alone is enough to turn a pauper into a rich person.
· Domino Effect Fallacy We cannot assume that one event will trigger another, which then triggers a series of subsequent events. The Domino Error is sometimes also called the &ldquo;Chain Reaction Fallacy.&rdquo; &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t oppose providing free food to the homeless, but since we provide free food, we will need to provide free clothes, and then free accommodation. Soon, we will have to provide them with a fixed annual salary.&rdquo;
· Gambler&rsquo;s Fallacy When we judge the probability of a future event not based on facts, but merely based on what happened previously—and these two events are completely independent and unrelated—we are committing the Gambler&rsquo;s Fallacy. (Parents to doctor) &ldquo;Since we already have three boys, I believe the next one must be a girl.&rdquo; The result of the last birth is actually completely unrelated to the result of the next birth; it is certain that the probability of the next birth being a boy or a girl is still 50/50.
· Fallacy of Precision What is meant by the Fallacy of Precision is arbitrarily citing figures that seem precise but are actually without factual basis to prove one&rsquo;s point. &ldquo;In the time of Shakespeare, one in every four people disliked Shakespeare&rsquo;s plays.&rdquo; The speaker is unlikely to obtain such precise data, especially data from the time of Shakespeare over four hundred years ago.

&ldquo;Mr. Willie&rsquo;s desk is always a mess because he is incompetent. A messy desk indicates a confused mind, which can only mean one thing: he is unfit for his job.&rdquo; This sentence is actually very simple: &ldquo;Incompetent, therefore the desk is messy; because the desk is messy, therefore he cannot be fit for his job.&rdquo; · Counter-argument When we conclude that something is true simply because it &ldquo;has not been proven false,&rdquo; or conclude that it is false simply because it &ldquo;has not been proven true,&rdquo; our judgment contains a logical flaw. For example, although no one has found evidence of Atlantis&rsquo;s existence yet, this does not mean that Atlantis does not exist.
Hasty Generalization When we rush to draw a conclusion based only on a small sample, we are likely to commit the fallacy of hasty generalization. &ldquo;I have been to Phoenix three times, and it rained every time, so Phoenix must be a rainy city.&rdquo; (In fact, Phoenix is a dry climate city.)
&ldquo;Because none of the company&rsquo;s interns have complained that the salary is too low, we can confidently say that the interns are satisfied with their salaries.&rdquo; · Personal Preference When we judge something based solely on personal emotions without considering the actual circumstances, we are committing the fallacy of personal preference. &ldquo;How can you consult Sheila about marriage problems? Don&rsquo;t you know she once went to jail because of email fraud?&rdquo;
· Poisoning the Well When we judge a person&rsquo;s statements too heavily based on their background, especially nationality, race, or gender, our logic becomes flawed. &ldquo;How can your words be taken seriously? You are from Sydney, so of course you would say Sydney is better than Melbourne.&rdquo;
· You&rsquo;re No Better Off
If we refuse to accept someone&rsquo;s viewpoint simply because they have made the same mistake as us, our logic is also flawed. Father: &ldquo;Son, you shouldn&rsquo;t drink. Drinking harms the liver; how can you be drunk all the time?&rdquo; Son: &ldquo;Dad, aren&rsquo;t you holding a glass of wine right now?&rdquo; Although the son&rsquo;s father is also drinking, this does not prove that his advice to his son is wrong.
· Red Herring
When we try to evade our weaknesses by changing the subject during a conversation, we are committing the &ldquo;Red Herring&rdquo; fallacy. (Boss to subordinate): &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t tell me the salary is too low. When I was your age, I only earned $100 a week.&rdquo; The fact that &ldquo;the boss earned little when young&rdquo; is irrelevant to the current conversation, because the boss&rsquo;s low salary when young does not mean the subordinate shouldn&rsquo;t get a raise.
Forcing an Irrelevant Goal or Function When we reject a rule or plan because it cannot satisfy an irrelevant goal, we are committing this logical error. Pete: &ldquo;Do you really think studying logic can solve the problems of this world?&rdquo; Tiffany: &ldquo;Probably not.&rdquo; Pete: &ldquo;Then why are we wasting time studying it?&rdquo; · Wishful Thinking When we assume something is true (or false) simply because we strongly wish it to be so, we are committing the &ldquo;Wishful Thinking&rdquo; fallacy. &ldquo;No matter how our team performed before, this time we will defeat the reigning champion in the first round. Our players are very confident and are putting in all their effort to achieve a great victory.&rdquo;
· Appeal to Tradition When we try to convince an opponent based on factors like seniority or tradition, rather than on facts or evidence, we are committing this logical error. &ldquo;Tim, don&rsquo;t take the idea of going to Annapolis too seriously! Your family—including your father, brothers, grandfather, uncle, etc.—have always been in the military, and they will continue to be in the service. So, young man, your future is at West Point!&rdquo; · Appeal to Popular Opinion When we accept or support a viewpoint simply because everyone agrees with it, we are committing this logical error. &ldquo;I am going to vote in favor of the tax law amendment. According to the results of a recent public opinion poll, more than two-thirds of registered voters under the age of 25 support the amendment.&rdquo; · Appeal to Pity What is called &ldquo;appealing to pity&rdquo; is the method of persuading someone by utilizing their sympathy, rather than factual evidence. (Sheila to John): &ldquo;You must donate to the orphanage. These children are born without knowing who their biological parents are, let alone having a secure life.&rdquo;

